WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.080
Batch size tuning for inference is the practice of choosing how much

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.060
work the runtime should process together when it serves prompts and generates

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:08.710
tokens.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.354
It sounds simple, but it sits at the intersection of model size, context length,

00:00:13.354 --> 00:00:15.012
memory, hardware, and user expectations.

00:00:15.012 --> 00:00:16.007
In practical terms,

00:00:16.007 --> 00:00:19.987
batch settings decide whether the system handles one request at a time,

00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:21.645
groups pieces of work together,

00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:25.294
or keeps multiple active requests moving through the model at once.

00:00:25.294 --> 00:00:27.615
This is different from training batch size.

00:00:27.615 --> 00:00:32.259
During training, a batch is tied to examples, loss calculation, gradients, and weight updates.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:34.912
During inference, the model weights are already fixed.

00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:36.239
The question becomes operational:

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:40.882
how can the runtime use available compute efficiently while still returning useful responses quickly?

00:00:40.882 --> 00:00:44.862
A setting that makes sense for training documentation may not translate directly

00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:47.516
to a chat server or a local assistant.

00:00:47.516 --> 00:00:50.832
It also helps to separate prompt processing from token generation.

00:00:50.832 --> 00:00:52.491
When a prompt is submitted,

00:00:52.491 --> 00:00:57.134
the runtime has to process the existing context before the first new token appears.

00:00:57.134 --> 00:00:59.788
After that, generation usually happens step by step.

00:00:59.788 --> 00:01:02.109
Batch settings can affect these phases differently,

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:06.753
so a configuration that improves prompt ingestion may not produce the smoothest streaming response.

00:01:06.753 --> 00:01:09.074
The right answer depends on the workload.

00:01:09.074 --> 00:01:13.054
A single-user local coding assistant usually values responsiveness and stable memory use.

00:01:13.054 --> 00:01:18.030
A multi-user API service may accept slightly more latency if it can increase total throughput.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.336
There is no universal best batch size because the best operating point changes with model architecture,

00:01:23.336 --> 00:01:26.321
quantization, runtime, hardware, prompt length, concurrency, and reliability goals.

00:01:26.321 --> 00:01:31.628
A practical way to think about this is to start with the user experience you want,

00:01:31.628 --> 00:01:33.950
then work backward to the runtime settings.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.271
If the system is a private assistant,

00:01:36.271 --> 00:01:39.588
the tuning target may be quick feedback and steady streaming.

00:01:39.588 --> 00:01:41.578
If it is an internal API,

00:01:41.578 --> 00:01:44.563
the target may be more completed requests per minute.

00:01:44.563 --> 00:01:49.220
Batch size is only meaningful when it is tied to one of those operating goals.
